A1. For the present this is a darned good place. ~ STOCKING
A8. This may be the current issue for April. ~ STREAM
A11. Vegetables with B.O. ~ ONIONS
A14. Paid for the drinks (obsolete, Scotch). ~ TREATED
A16. One of the touching things about France. ~ SPAIN
A17. First he dosed and then he dozed till 20 years had passed. ~ RIP
A18. French pep. ~ ELAN
A20. Decorate the sheets. ~ SLEEP
A22. What sturdy Scotchmen never do. ~ GIVE
A23. Change the alarm. ~ RESET
A25. Just a little remembrance from Major Operation. ~ SCAR
A26. The right key does this to the right flat. ~ OPENS
A28. Of whom they sing (and I don't mean the big, bad wolf). ~ THEE
A29. Squeeze out. ~ EKE
A30. If you wish to make a name for yourself, here is a capital idea. ~ LETTER
A32. A vestment. ~ ALB
A35. Sorry, the original is all torn up, so try the next one. (abbr.) ~ ST
A36. This is dry and uninteresting. ~ SERE
A37. A nincompoop. ~ LOUT
A39. This goes with Santa when he is on the way to California. ~ FE
A41. It takes someone who has been calm and collected to do this. ~ DUN
A42. A tavern now in our town. ~ SALOON
A44. A beheaded king. ~ ING
A46. The moving spirit of the times. ~ MOTOR
A50. Helps any little donkey to "git along." ~ FLOGS
A51. It gives good service with no tipping. ~ SCOW
A53. Followed suit. ~ APED
A55. Oh! to be in Paris Eastertime. ~ ETRE
A56. Speaking of the judge, this may be his. ~ HONOR
A58. Where you get a pain in the neck. ~ NAPE
A59. A little article for particular people. ~ THE
A60. Bill's last word. ~ REMIT
A62. These give one an entrance into the best hotels. ~ LOBBIES
A64. What to use for a little change. ~ ERASER
A66. These have always been upright and steady and yet many newspapers lie about them. ~ STANDS
A67. The one, we hope Santa Claus treated the best last Xmas. ~ NEEDIEST

D1. Wouldn't this make you sick, nothing but chops and rolls. ~ SEA
D2. A vegetable prepared in southern style. ~ TATER
D3. Humpty Dumpty wound up in this after his fall. ~ OMELET
D4. This is correct if you get the right point of view. ~ KO
D5. These are always lucky on a rainy day. ~ INS
D6. These come with the real New Year's spirit. ~ NIPS
D7. Constant kickers get these. ~ GOALS
D8. A line from the pen. ~ STRIPE
D9. These are very right. ~ TRIVETS
D10. What the 1931 penthouse owner did in 1932. ~ REPENTED
D12. This is of relative importance. ~ NIECE
D13. Those who peek at their presents before Xmas. ~ SNEAKS
D15. Dot's companion. ~ DASH
D19. A bare knee. ~ NEE
D21. This is stuck on at parties. ~ PRETENSE
D22. A herring on the gold standard. ~ GOLDFISH
D24. Ducks. ~ TEALS
D27. It must be in the blood. ~ SERUM
D31. The great divide of the United States. ~ RENO
D33. College bred. ~ LOAF
D34. A news flash. ~ BULLETIN
D38. Ready for the grind. ~ TOOTHED
D40. A dramatic comeback. ~ ENCORE
D43. A female bugbear. ~ OGRESS
D45. That dear departed friend. ~ GONER
D47. A Palm Beach coat. ~ TAN
D48. The flashy one of the set. ~ OPAL
D49. To couch in other words. ~ REPOSE
D52. This one is no gentleman. ~ WOMAN
D54. What Santa Claus often brings to the head of the household. ~ DEBTS
D57. This is on the up and up. ~ RISE
D61. This might start a strike. ~ TEE
D63. How would this kind of a diamond ornament strike you? ~ BAT
D65. This might lead to almost anything, even the White House. (abbr.) ~ RD


